722	    /**
723	     * Works out the late fee and late interest charged by the given day, at the late fee (in US
724	     * dollars) and annual rate (a percentage), from the payments (those not reversed count), and keeps
725	     * them as part of what is payable. With a late fee set (whatever its amount) the fee is 2% of what the
726	     * invoice still owes when it is charged (see {@link #lateFeeOn}), never more than 10% of the grand
727	     * total (see {@link #lateFeeCap}). The fee is charged once, on the day the invoice becomes overdue
728	     * ({@link #graceDays()} days after its due date), when something is still owed then; it earns no
729	     * interest, and a payment from then on pays it once the invoice itself is paid. A sent invoice not
730	     * spread over instalments, with something still owed more than {@link #graceDays()} days after its
731	     * due date, earns interest from the end of its grace period ({@link #graceDays()} days after its due
732	     * date), compounded daily at rate / 360 (Actual/360): a balance B owing for
733	     * d days earns B x ((1 + rate / 100 / 360)^d - 1), rounded half up to the cent. Each payment after
734	     * the grace period ends a period; every period earns interest on what the invoice still owed during it
735	     * (earlier interest, the fee and retention left out), and a payment pays the invoice, then the fee,
736	     * then the interest earned so far. A credit note issued after the grace period ends a period too:
737	     * what it took off what the invoice owed counts only from its issue date, so the periods before it
738	     * earn interest on what was owed before the credit. An invoice spread over instalments instead earns interest
739	     * instalment by instalment and is charged the late fee once on each instalment (see
740	     * {@link #accrueInstalmentInterest}). On the day the
741	     * charges were waived (after the day's payments), the fee and interest still unpaid are written off
742	     * (kept as what was waived) and that day ends a period: interest starts again from it on what is
743	     * still owed, and no new fee is charged. A written-off invoice is charged only up to the day it was
744	     * written off. Released retention still unpaid also earns interest (see
745	     * {@link #accrueRetentionInterest}).
746	     */
747	    public void accrueLateInterest(List<Payment> payments, BigDecimal taxRate, BigDecimal annualRate,
748	            BigDecimal lateFeeUsd, LocalDate asOf) {
749	        annualRate = lateInterestRate(annualRate);
750	        LocalDate today = writtenOffOn != null && writtenOffOn.isBefore(asOf) ? writtenOffOn : asOf;
751	        this.lateInterest = BigDecimal.ZERO;
752	        this.lateFee = BigDecimal.ZERO;
753	        this.lateWaived = BigDecimal.ZERO;
754	        this.retentionInterest = BigDecimal.ZERO;
755	        this.instalmentInterest = List.of();
756	        this.instalmentFees = List.of();
757	        if (status == InvoiceStatus.SENT && !isCreditNote() && hasPlan()) {
758	            accrueInstalmentInterest(payments, taxRate, annualRate, lateFeeUsd, today);
759	            return;
760	        }
761	        accrueInvoiceInterest(payments, taxRate, annualRate, lateFeeUsd, today);
762	        accrueRetentionInterest(payments, taxRate, annualRate, today);
763	    }
